Here's the complete project — all tests pass (tools, static resources, and templated resources all respond correctly):

**Program.cs** — wires up MCP with HTTP Streamable transport:

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services
    .AddMcpServer()
    .WithHttpTransport()        // Streamable HTTP transport
    .WithTools<WeatherTools>()
    .WithResources<ConfigResources>();

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapMcp();                   // Maps MCP endpoints at "/"

app.Run();
```

**ConfigResources.cs** — exposes a static resource and a URI-templated resource:

```csharp
[McpServerResourceType]
public class ConfigResources
{
    // Static resource: config://app
    [McpServerResource(UriTemplate = "config://app", Name = "app-config",
        MimeType = "application/json")]
    public static string GetAppConfig() { ... }

    // Templated resource: config://app/{section}
    [McpServerResource(UriTemplate = "config://app/{section}",
        Name = "app-config-section", MimeType = "application/json")]
    public static string GetConfigSection(string section) { ... }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `WithHttpTransport()` + `MapMcp()` sets up the **Streamable HTTP** transport (MCP 2025-03-26 spec)
- `[McpServerResourceType]` marks a class for resource discovery; `[McpServerResource]` marks individual resource methods
- Static URIs (no `{param}`) appear in `resources/list`; templated URIs appear in `resources/templates/list`
- The method parameter name (`section`) must match the URI template placeholder

The project is at `McpHttpServer/` and builds cleanly with `dotnet build`.
